### 🔌 Hardware Pinout & Wiring Connections

| Component | Component Pin | ESP32-C6 Pin | Connection Notes & Function |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **MOSFET Module** | Gate (SIG) | **GPIO 18** | Drives PWM signal for variable load dimming / throttling (LED / Fan)[cite: 1] |
| | VCC / GND | External / GND | Connected to external load power supply circuit |
| **5V Relay Module** | IN (Signal) | **GPIO 19** | Acts as main circuit breaker drive (High-Impedance Active-Low)[cite: 1] |
| | VCC | **5V / VBUS** | Powered via 5V line from USB-C supply[cite: 1] |
| | GND | **GND** | Connected to common system ground |
| **Push Button** | Terminal 1 | **GPIO 2** | Short Press = Mesh Relief; Long Press = Blackout Toggle[cite: 1] |
| | Terminal 2 | **GND** | Configured with internal `PULLUP` resistor |
| **Status LED** | Anode (+) | **GPIO 20** | Visual indicator for Thread mesh network attachment[cite: 1] |
| | Cathode (-) | **GND** | Connected via 220Ω current-limiting resistor |

> **Note:** Both **Node 1** (Local Load Controller) and **Node 2** (Remote Mesh Responder) share the exact same hardware pinout setup for consistent peer-to-peer mesh operation[cite: 1]. Ensure all ground (GND) lines across power supplies, modules, and microcontrollers are tied to a common ground point.
